          [{ "text": "7" }],
          [{ "text": "Bdim", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "B D F", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "diminished" }]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "There's a compact way to name these seven chords that works in every major key, not just C, and it's worth learning properly. A Roman numeral names a chord by the scale degree it's built on, so the numeral "
        },
        { "text": "I", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " means \"the chord on the first degree,\" whatever the key is — " },
        { "text": "I", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " in C major is " },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "I", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " in G major is " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Case carries quality, and nothing else: uppercase is major, lowercase is minor, lowercase with a small circle is diminished — "
        },
        { "text": "I ii iii IV V vi vii°", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One correction worth making explicitly, because it's an easy one to get backwards: "
        },
        { "text": "V", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is uppercase because the chord built on " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is major — not because " },
        { "text": "V", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " is somehow the most important chord in the key. Case tracks what the chord sounds like, not what job it does in the progression."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The sequence of qualities is fixed for every major key: major, minor, minor, major, major, minor, diminished. Run the same construction in G major and the result is "
        },
        { "text": "G Am Bm C D Em F#dim", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — the identical pattern, four letters over. Learn the pattern once and you can build the seven chords of any major key straight from its key signature."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "This also answers a question chapter 4 raised without fully explaining: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bdim",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-the-flat-fifth" }
        },
        {
          "text": " is the one diminished triad whose notes are entirely inside C major, and no augmented triad fits inside a major scale at all. Both are true for the same reason — "
        },
        { "text": "Bdim", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " is simply the triad built on the seventh degree, and a major scale never places a flat fifth or a sharp fifth on any other degree."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's what the pattern looks like in your hands. Harmonise the whole scale on strings 1-2-3, keeping every chord in "
        },
        { "text": "second inversion", "marks": ["bold"] },
        { "text": " — the same rotation throughout, nothing else changing. The starting grip, " },
        { "text": "x x x 0 1 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", is the very first shape this pathway taught." }
      ]
    },
    {
