    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Two things this run makes visible. First: the bass voice, string 3, walks the scale itself — "
        },
        { "text": "G A B C D E F G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " at frets " },
        { "text": "0 2 4 5 7 9 10 12", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". It's the fifth of every chord, because second inversion always puts the fifth in the bass. For "
        },
        { "text": "Bdim", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " that fifth is the flatted one, " },
        { "text": "F", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the same rule holding even where the chord itself is unusual." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [{ "text": "Second: every voice moves up by one or two frets and never more." }]
    },
    {
