    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Only second inversion fits this run on this neck, and that's worth saying plainly rather than hiding. Root position would start at fret 5 and need "
        },
        { "text": "Bdim", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " at fret 16; first inversion starts at fret 9 and needs " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " at fret 16 too. The neck has 15 frets, so both run off the end. Second inversion starts at the nut for a specific reason: its bass note is always the fifth, and the fifth of "
        },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the open third string." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Loop it against the "
        },
        { "text": "Metronome", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/metronome" } },
        {
          "text": ", one chord per bar, slow, up the scale and back down — say the numeral out loud rather than the letter, since the numeral is the name that survives a key change. Then look at the "
        },
        { "text": "Scale Visualizer", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/scale-visualizer" } },
        {
          "text": " to see the scale these seven chords are built from. It draws the scale, not the grips, so use it for that and nothing else."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Seven chords with numbers on them is what makes a progression something you can carry into another key. Which one you actually play, and on which strings, is a separate question — and it's the next one."
        }
      ]
    }
